{"name":"Turtle Bay","hex":"#84897f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"turtle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any land, aquatic, or semi-aquatic reptile of the order Testudines, characterised by a protective shell enclosing its body. See also tortoise.","A marine reptile of that order."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To flip over onto the back or top; to turn upside down.\n\nTo turn and swim upside down.\n\n To capsize, turning fully over.","To turn and swim upside down."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/turtle"},{"term":"bay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Batuley."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A body of water (especially the sea) contained by a concave shoreline.","A bank or dam to keep back water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Turtle Bay, Manhattan","blocks":[{"text":"Turtle Bay is a neighborhood in New York City, on the east side of Midtown Manhattan. It extends from roughly 43rd Street to 53rd Street, and eastward from Lexington Avenue to the East River's western branch. The neighborhood is the site of the headquarters of the United Nations and the Chrysler Building. The Tudor City apartment complex is next to the southeast corner of Turtle Bay."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Townhouses2.jpg/330px-Townhouses2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turtle_Bay%2C_Manhattan","wikidata":"Q976477"},{"title":"Turtle Bay, Oʻahu","blocks":[{"text":"Turtle Bay is located between Protection Point and Kuilima Point on the North Shore of the island of Oʻahu in the U.S. State of Hawaii."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Turtle_Bay%2C_Hawai%27i_-_panoramio.jpg/330px-Turtle_Bay%2C_Hawai%27i_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turtle_Bay%2C_O%CA%BBahu","wikidata":"Q7856372"},{"title":"Turtle","blocks":[{"text":"Turtles are reptiles characterized by a special shell developed mainly from their ribs. Modern turtles are divided into two major groups, the Pleurodira and Cryptodira, which differ in the way the head retracts. There are 360 living and recently extinct species of turtles, including land-dwelling tortoises and freshwater terrapins. They are found on most continents, some islands and, in the case of sea turtles, much of the ocean. Like other amniotes they breathe air and do not lay eggs underwater, although many species live in or around water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Turtle_diversity.jpg/330px-Turtle_diversity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turtle","wikidata":"Q223044"},{"title":"Bay","blocks":[{"text":"A bay is a recessed, coastal body of water that directly connects to a larger main body of water, such as an ocean, a lake, or another bay. A large bay is usually called a gulf, sea, sound, or bight. A cove is a small, circular bay with a narrow entrance. A fjord is an elongated bay formed by glacial action.\nThe term embayment is also used for related features, such as extinct bays or freshwater environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg/330px-Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay","wikidata":"Q39594"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Turtle_Bay%2C_Manhattan_1853.jpg/500px-Turtle_Bay%2C_Manhattan_1853.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn